### I CANNOT SOLVE THIS

The first part of this problem — "Does every compact space without isolated points admit an irreducible map onto a continuum?" — is a well-known **open problem** in set-theoretic topology, attributed to Eric van Douwen. It appears in standard problem lists (e.g., van Douwen's problem list, and references in van Mill's work) and, to the best of my knowledge, has not been resolved in ZFC.

The second part — whether $\omega^* = \beta\omega \setminus \omega$ admits an irreducible map onto a continuum — has a positive answer **under CH**, as noted in the problem statement. This is a deep result in set-theoretic topology whose proof relies on the special structure that $\omega^*$ acquires under the Continuum Hypothesis (existence and density of P-points, careful transfinite constructions of continuous functions $\beta\omega \to [0,1]$ whose restrictions to $\omega^*$ are irreducible surjections onto a continuum). I am not confident I can reconstruct this proof correctly and rigorously from memory, and I will not fabricate one.

Since the primary question is open and I cannot reliably reproduce the known partial result for the second part, I cannot solve this problem.
